## Verdict

SOLVED: no

A credible value characterization is available for finite public-exogenous classes, and likely for infinite classes under payoff-weighted domination/no-gap hypotheses. But the original goal of a natural scalar tail characterization with matching regret rates is still open, and raw \(\beta\), \(\Delta\), or \(\delta\) profiles cannot be the whole story.

## Candidate Solution Or Main Attempt

For finite horizon \(T\), public exogenous laws \(\Theta\), finite quote grid \(G\), and randomized policies, define
\[
M_t=\max_{s>t}P_s,\qquad
V_\theta^G=\sup_{a\in G}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb E_\theta[a{\bf 1}\{M_t>a\}].
\]
For finite \(F\subseteq \Theta\) and prior \(\pi\),
\[
\Psi_{G,F}(\pi)=
\sum_{\theta\in F}\pi_\theta V_\theta^G
-\sum_{t=1}^T\mathbb E_{\bar P_\pi}\max_{a\in G}
a\,\bar P_\pi(M_t>a\mid P_{1:t}).
\]

Accepted finite-class theorem:
\[
\mathcal R_G(\Theta)
=
\inf_A\sup_{\theta\in\Theta}(V_\theta^G-L_\theta(A))
=
\sup_{\pi\in\Delta(\Theta)}\Psi_{G,\Theta}(\pi).
\]
With \(G_T=\{0,1/T,\ldots,(T-1)/T\}\),
\[
\left|
(\mathcal R_T^{[0,1]})_+
-
\left(\sup_{\pi\in\Delta(\Theta_T)}\Psi_{G_T,\Theta_T}(\pi)\right)_+
\right|\le 1.
\]
So finite-class sublinear upper regret is equivalent to this Bayes predictability gap being \(o(T)\).

The clean sufficient upper bound remains:
\[
R_T\lesssim \sqrt{T(H+1)\log K}+T/K+\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_T}\delta_T^\theta(H).
\]
If \(\Delta_T(H)\le TH^{-\alpha}\), this gives
\[
R_T\lesssim T^{(\alpha+1)/(2\alpha+1)}(\log T)^{\alpha/(2\alpha+1)}.
\]

## Concrete Lemmas Or Reductions

1. The remaining dominated infinite-class alignment looks provable under the exact finite-grid public-exogenous Borel model. Every private-randomized Borel policy has a seed representation
\[
a_t=\alpha_t(P_{1:t},U_{1:t}),
\]
so it induces a law-independent public-prefix kernel
\[
q_{t,a}(h)=\Pr_U(\alpha_t(h,U_{1:t})=a)
\]
with
\[
L_\theta(A)=\sum_{t,a}\int q_{t,a}(h)\,d\mu^\theta_{t,a}(h).
\]

2. Conversely, if \(q_t\in L^\infty(\lambda_t;\Delta(G))\) and \(\mu^\theta_{t,a}\ll\lambda_t\), standard Borel version theorems give Borel simplex-valued representatives equal \(\lambda_t\)-a.e.; these preserve all reward integrals and are implementable with fresh uniforms.

3. Hence payoff-weighted domination
\[
\mu^\theta_{t,a}\ll \lambda_t
\]
should upgrade the completed-kernel no-gap theorem to original Borel policies:
\[
\mathcal R_G(\Theta)
=
\sup_{F\Subset\Theta}\sup_{\pi\in\Delta(F)}\Psi_{G,F}(\pi).
\]

4. New useful obstruction: tail-profile no-go pair. Take the \(q\)-scaled hidden-terminal two-world class. Now add a harmless label in \(P_1\) revealing the world before all payoff-relevant quote decisions, keeping future prices identical. Per-world \(\beta,\Delta,\delta\) profiles are unchanged, but minimax regret drops from \(\Omega(qD)\) to \(0\). Thus individual tail profiles cannot characterize unknown-class learnability; observability/predictability is essential.

## Gaps And Failure Points

No arbitrary infinite-class theorem without product-closed/no-gap assumptions.

No scalar necessary-and-sufficient tail condition is known, and the tail-profile no-go pair suggests none can depend only on individual laws’ delay/truncation profiles.

No matching lower bound for the power-tail upper rate is known.

Action-dependent prices and anytime horizon-free policies remain separate unsolved regimes.

## Counterexamples Or Obstructions

Raw \(\beta(h)\to0\) is not necessary: known finite-horizon exogenous laws are oracle-learnable with \(R_T\le T/K\).

\(\Delta_T(H)=o(T)\) is not necessary for known laws: terminal-spike laws have zero oracle regret but linear truncation loss.

Rare-history and tiny-price examples have \(\beta(h)=1\) for all finite \(h\), yet bounded expected regret.

Non-Borel label classes show finite-subclass Bayes values need not characterize arbitrary infinite Borel-policy classes.

## Promising Ideas To Explore

Write the policy-alignment lemmas in theorem-ready form.

Use \(\Psi_G\) to search for multi-scale lower bounds where information does not collapse after one reveal.

Develop the tail-profile no-go pair into a formal impossibility theorem for scalar tail characterizations.

Keep adaptive truncation upper bounds and matching power-tail lower bounds as separate projects.

## Notes For Critics

Check the policy model carefully: public prices, finite grid, independent private randomness, and feedback determined by public prices plus past quotes.

Keep grid comparator, learner actions, and Bayes envelope on the same \(G\).

Use \(\sup_a\), not \(\max_a\), unless attainment is proved.

The result is a minimax/Bayes value characterization, not a tail iff theorem.
